---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';

/**
 * SEO / compatibility page, same shape and same rules as
 * stronglifts-5x5-tracker.astro. Read that file's header before editing this
 * one; the reasoning is identical.
 *
 * TRADEMARK HYGIENE, binding: FitNotes is someone else's app. Everything here
 * is phrased as compatibility (Badger reads the backup file their app writes),
 * never as affiliation or endorsement, and never as a criticism of their app.
 * FitNotes users like FitNotes. A page that opens by running it down loses the
 * reader in one sentence, and would deserve to.
 *
 * Claims about what the import carries were checked against the app source on
 * 2026-09-05, against lib/features/import/domain/fitnotes_parser.dart and
 * fitnotes_importer.dart:
 *   - categories: recreated by name WITH their FitNotes color, reused when a
 *     category of that name already exists (_doImport step 1)
 *   - exercises: matched by name, then by the reviewed mapping plan, else
 *     created in their original category with the FitNotes exercise type
 *     mapped across (step 2)
 *   - sets: reps, weight, distance, time, comment; both the modern
 *     `workout_entry` schema and the pre-2018 `training_log` one, where the
 *     kg-canonical column is turned back into the unit it was typed in
 *   - body weight: appended to the Body Weight measurement type
 *   - custom measurements: their own types, carrying the unit string
 * There is deliberately no warmup claim: FitNotes does not mark warmups, so
 * the importer has nothing to read and every set arrives as a working set.
 */

const description =
  'Move to Badger from FitNotes without losing anything. Your .fitnotes backup brings the whole library across: categories, exercises, every set, body weight, and your custom measurements.';

const modified = '2026-09-05';
---

<HelpLayout title="Moving from FitNotes" description={description}>
  <ArticleSchema
    slot="head"
    headline="Moving to Badger from FitNotes"
    description={description}
    dateModified={modified}
    about={['FitNotes', 'Workout tracking']}
  />

  <p class="crumb"><a href="/guides/switching">Switching to Badger</a></p>

  <h1>Moving to Badger from FitNotes</h1>

  <p>
    FitNotes people are usually not shopping because they hate FitNotes. They liked that it was fast,
    free, offline, and did not want anything from them. That is the same list Badger was built
    against, so the question worth answering here is a narrow one: does your history come with you,
    and what do you get that you did not have?
  </p>

  <h2 id="import">What the backup file carries across</h2>

  <p>
    FitNotes backs up to a <code>.fitnotes</code> file, which is a real database rather than a
    flattened CSV. That is lucky for you, because it means far more than your sets survives the move.
    Badger reads the file directly, on your phone, with nothing uploaded anywhere.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your categories, with their colors.</strong> The groups you sorted your exercises into
      come over as Badger categories, keeping the color you gave each one. A category you already
      have is reused rather than duplicated.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your exercise library, in those categories.</strong> Not dumped in an "Imported"
      bucket. Each exercise lands where you had it, and its FitNotes type (weight and reps, cardio,
      and so on) is carried across so the logging screen shows the right fields.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every set</strong>, with its date, weight, reps, distance, time, and any comment you
      left on it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Old backups too.</strong> FitNotes changed its database layout around 2018. Badger
      reads both, and on the older one it turns the stored kilograms back into the unit you actually
      typed, so a pounds log stays a pounds log rather than arriving as 102.06.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your body weight history</strong>, appended to Badger's own body weight track.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your custom measurements</strong>, each becoming its own measurement type and keeping
      its unit. Waist, arms, whatever you were tracking.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>PRs, recalculated.</strong> Not read from the file, worked out again from the history
      once it lands, so your records and graphs start out full.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>What does not come across, so nothing surprises you later:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Warmups, as warmups.</strong> FitNotes does not mark them, so there is nothing for the
      importer to read. Every logged set arrives as a working set. You can flag them afterwards, but
      nothing can guess retroactively.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Routines.</strong> The import brings history and your exercise library. Programs are a
      separate thing you set up in Badger, and there are templates to start from.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Anything else in the file.</strong> The importer reads the tables listed above and
      leaves the rest alone rather than guessing at it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="how">Doing it</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>In FitNotes, go to <strong>Settings &rarr; Backup</strong> to write a <code>.fitnotes</code> file.</li>
    <li>
      Get the file onto the phone you train with, however you normally move a file: email it to
      yourself, drop it in cloud storage, use a cable.
    </li>
    <li>
      In Badger, go to <strong>Settings &rarr; Your data &rarr; Import from FitNotes</strong> and pick
      the file. It is also offered during first-launch onboarding.
    </li>
    <li>Check the preview: exercises, workout days, sets, and the detected weight unit.</li>
    <li>Import.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    It runs more than once safely. Sets landing on a date you already have merge into that session,
    and nothing already logged is overwritten, so adding a newer backup later is fine. Full detail in
    <a href="/help/getting-started#importing-fitnotes">importing from FitNotes</a> and
    <a href="/guides/switching">switching from another app</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="whats-different">What you get that you did not have</h2>

  <p>
    Worth being concrete, because "it has more features" is not a reason to move a decade of
    training log.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Routines that pre-fill.</strong> Build your program once and each session arrives with
      your exercises and last weights already in place.
      <a href="/programs">Seven programs ship built in</a>, including StrongLifts 5x5, GZCLP and PPL.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gym and machine tracking.</strong> Record which gym and which machine a set was done
      on, and get PRs scoped to that machine, because the leg press at one gym is not the leg press
      at another. <a href="/help/gyms">How that works</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A plate calculator and a warmup generator</strong> that both work from the plates your
      gym actually owns, and round to loads you can really make.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A rest timer that starts itself</strong> when you complete a set, per exercise.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Graphs worth opening.</strong> Estimated 1RM, eleven graphs per exercise, an activity
      heatmap, weekly volume by muscle group, and plateau detection.
      <a href="/help/progress">What it tracks</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It runs on iPhone.</strong> Badger is on Google Play, with an iOS beta open in
      TestFlight, so a phone switch does not mean an app switch.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    And the part that is the same on purpose: no account, no sign-in, no sync you did not ask for.
    Your log lives on your phone. <a href="/promise">The promise</a> is that written down where you
    can hold us to it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="getting-out">Getting back out again</h2>

  <p>
    The reason to trust an import is knowing you can leave. Badger exports every set to CSV, and
    backs the whole database up to a <a href="/badger-format">documented file format</a> that is
    ordinary readable text. Neither is a paid feature, and neither needs an account. If Badger turns
    out not to suit you, you leave with your numbers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cost">What it costs</h2>

  <p>
    Nothing. No account, no subscription, no ads, no cap on routines or on how far back your history
    goes. <a href="/guides/workout-tracker-no-subscription">The no-subscription guide</a> compares
    what free actually means across the trackers people usually weigh up, Badger included.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trademark">About the name</h2>

  <p class="fineprint">
    FitNotes is the work of its own developer, and Badger is not affiliated with, endorsed by,
    sponsored by, or connected to it in any way. This page describes compatibility and nothing else:
    Badger reads the backup file that their app writes. FitNotes is a good app and this is not an
    argument that it is not one.
  </p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  /* The section this page sits in, named above the h1 so a reader landing here
     from a search knows there are sibling pages for the other apps. */
  .crumb {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* Matches the StrongLifts page: a disclaimer should read as one rather than
     competing with the guidance above it. Muted is AA-checked in both themes. */
  .fineprint {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
